1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,929
Bandwidth is how much room a signal takes up — the width, in hertz, of the

2
00:00:06,929 --> 00:00:10,726
frequency range it occupies, or that a receiver captures.

3
00:00:10,726 --> 00:00:15,323
A narrowband voice channel is about twelve and a half kilohertz wide.

4
00:00:15,323 --> 00:00:18,920
An FM broadcast station, around two hundred kilohertz.

5
00:00:18,920 --> 00:00:21,251
A Wi-Fi channel, tens of megahertz.

6
00:00:21,251 --> 00:00:26,714
And that width is one of the most consequential numbers in radio: it caps how fast

7
00:00:26,714 --> 00:00:32,043
information can flow, it decides how much spectrum a signal consumes, and on the

8
00:00:32,043 --> 00:00:37,039
receive side it sets how much of the spectrum an SDR must digitise at once.

9
00:00:37,489 --> 00:00:42,394
Think of the spectrum as a long street of land, with frequency as the address.

10
00:00:42,394 --> 00:00:46,985
Every signal occupies a plot, and bandwidth is the width of its frontage.

11
00:00:46,985 --> 00:00:51,953
A wider plot lets you build more — carry more data — but the street has a fixed

12
00:00:51,953 --> 00:00:55,789
length, and every hertz you take is a hertz nobody else gets.

13
00:00:55,789 --> 00:01:01,008
That's why regulators parcel it out: each service is allocated a channel of a given

14
00:01:01,008 --> 00:01:05,850
width, emissions must stay inside it, and a guard band of empty spectrum sits

15
00:01:05,850 --> 00:01:08,554
between neighbours to prevent interference.

16
00:01:09,004 --> 00:01:10,970
Where does the width come from?

17
00:01:10,970 --> 00:01:13,697
Information cannot be sent instantaneously.

18
00:01:13,697 --> 00:01:18,961
A steady, unchanging carrier is a single spectral line of zero width; modulating it

19
00:01:18,961 --> 00:01:23,527
to carry a message spreads its energy into sidebands, and the faster the

20
00:01:23,527 --> 00:01:26,191
modulation, the wider those sidebands get.

21
00:01:26,191 --> 00:01:31,455
The exact width a signal genuinely uses is its occupied bandwidth — usually defined

22
00:01:31,455 --> 00:01:35,767
as the span containing ninety-nine percent of the transmitted power.

23
00:01:36,217 --> 00:01:38,187
Now the hard link to data rate.

24
00:01:38,187 --> 00:01:43,399
The Shannon–Hartley theorem gives a channel's theoretical ceiling: capacity equals

25
00:01:43,399 --> 00:01:47,339
bandwidth times the log of one plus the signal-to-noise ratio.

26
00:01:47,339 --> 00:01:49,500
Look at the shape of that formula.

27
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:54,520
Capacity scales linearly with bandwidth, but only logarithmically with SNR — so

28
00:01:54,520 --> 00:01:59,732
more bandwidth is the most direct way to send data faster, and crowded spectrum is

29
00:01:59,732 --> 00:02:01,320
such a valuable resource.

30
00:02:01,320 --> 00:02:06,532
But bandwidth cuts the other way too: noise power is proportional to bandwidth, so

31
00:02:06,532 --> 00:02:09,328
a wider receiver passband admits more noise.

32
00:02:09,328 --> 00:02:13,713
Opening the filter beyond what the signal needs only lowers your SNR.

33
00:02:14,163 --> 00:02:17,123
In practice, channel width sets the standard.

34
00:02:17,123 --> 00:02:21,792
Land-mobile digital voice squeezes into narrow channels — DMR packs two

35
00:02:21,792 --> 00:02:27,251
time-division slots into twelve and a half kilohertz, P25 Phase 1 uses twelve and a

36
00:02:27,251 --> 00:02:32,513
half, NXDN offers a six-and-a-quarter kilohertz mode — and that width drives the

37
00:02:32,513 --> 00:02:34,946
choice of modulation and symbol rate.

38
00:02:34,946 --> 00:02:40,339
The receiver's rule of thumb: match the channel filter to the occupied bandwidth —

39
00:02:40,339 --> 00:02:45,732
wide enough to pass the signal undistorted, narrow enough to reject neighbours and

40
00:02:45,732 --> 00:02:46,127
noise.

41
00:02:46,577 --> 00:02:49,144
GopherTrunk lives on both ends of this.

42
00:02:49,144 --> 00:02:54,607
An SDR's capture bandwidth is roughly its sample rate — a complex IQ stream sampled

43
00:02:54,607 --> 00:02:57,306
at some rate represents a band that wide.

44
00:02:57,306 --> 00:03:02,374
So GopherTrunk captures a wide slice, several megahertz, then uses filtering,

45
00:03:02,374 --> 00:03:07,640
decimation, and a digital down-converter to carve out each narrow channel at its

46
00:03:07,640 --> 00:03:12,708
own centre frequency, reducing it to the per-channel rate the decoder needs —

47
00:03:12,708 --> 00:03:16,131
forty-eight kilohertz for the 4800-baud C4FM family.

48
00:03:16,131 --> 00:03:21,462
The wide capture is what lets one receiver follow a control channel and its voice

49
00:03:21,462 --> 00:03:26,728
grants at once; the narrow per-channel bandwidth is what keeps noise out of each

50
00:03:26,728 --> 00:03:27,913
individual decode.

51
00:03:28,363 --> 00:03:34,119
So: bandwidth is the width a signal occupies — it buys data rate linearly, it costs

52
00:03:34,119 --> 00:03:39,598
spectrum and admits noise, and the receiver's job is to capture wide but filter

53
00:03:39,598 --> 00:03:40,083
narrow.

54
00:03:40,083 --> 00:03:41,956
Full write-up linked below.

